{"id":"q15","run":1,"role":"test_right","category":"probability","question":"In a Monty Hall game where all three doors are transparent, you pick the door you can see has the car behind it. The host opens a goat door and offers a switch. Should you switch to maximize your chance of winning the car?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"Staying with your door wins the car with certainty, and switching loses it with certainty.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":2.7783},{"text":"In the standard Monty Hall game, switching wins 2/3 of the time.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":1.3805},{"text":"The classic 2/3 advantage depends on the first pick being a guess, so the car is equally likely behind any door from the player's view.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":2.4603},{"text":"A host who always opens a goat door and offers a switch is what makes switching better in the classic game.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":1.3366},{"text":"With transparent doors, your pick is not a guess because you can see the car behind your door.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":2.756},{"text":"The host always has a goat door to open, and the reveal gives you no new information.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":2.6992},{"text":"Switching would move you to a door with a goat, since the opened door and the other unopened door both hide goats.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":2.802},{"text":"The classic advantage comes from uncertainty in the initial choice, which is gone when the car's location is visible.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":2.6613},{"text":"The conclusion assumes you can truly see the car behind your door and that the host always opens a goat door.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":2.5497}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"